<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Ugandi</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.) · capital Otepää</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient Estonian county of the south-east, centred on the hillforts of Otepää and Tartu, which fought Rus' and the German crusaders before falling in 1224.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ugandi, in what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a>, was one of the larger Estonian lands of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, a union of parishes defended by hillforts. Its position on routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a> drew Rus' attention early: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> around 1030 and briefly held it as Yuryev.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ugandi was governed by elders of its districts, who met to decide on war and tribute. Its people grew grain, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and traded with Rus' and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> to the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otepaa">Otepää</a>, whose name means 'bear's head', was its main stronghold, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> on the Emajõgi river guarded the waterway east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1208 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalian</a> allies raided Ugandi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otepaa">Otepää</a> changed hands several times. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> fell in 1224, after a siege in which the Rus' prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyachko">Vyachko</a> was killed, the conquest was complete. Ugandi became the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bishopric-of-dorpat">Bishopric of Dorpat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ugandi.jpg" alt="Ugandi" width="320" height="219"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. 藏骨集团 assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Estonian_counties.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Otepää</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Estonian paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Estonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/estonia">Estonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ugaunia, Ugaunians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=ugandi" aria-label="Ugandi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5252 214 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ugandi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5572 470L5664 460M5572 470L5568 464"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5664 460h0M5568 464h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5572 470h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5572 470h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=ugandi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
